From: CarPlay C0 lane (2026-11-22)
Subject: 0520 sweet_nub: account the manually built instance in its metaclass

0519 proved the construction itself works: the probe printed
  manual construction of IOUSBDeviceController: 504 bytes at 0xfffffff94fc5d000
  manual -> 0xfffffff94fc5d000, own class IOUSBDeviceController
and the S2 gate passed for the first time (S0=True, S2=True). One millisecond later the kernel
died at the S2-only path's release():

  OSMetaClass: Class IOUSBDeviceController - bad retain (-1)
  Break 0x0001 instruction exception from kernel. Panic (by design)

The generated class constructor does two things, and the manual path only did one: it set the
object retain count (retain()) but never called gMetaClass.instanceConstructed(). With the
metaclass count still 0, the first release() drives it to -1 and OSMetaClass panics. 0520 calls
mc->instanceConstructed() right after retain(), so construction/destruction accounting matches
what `new IOUSBDeviceController` would have done.

Device evidence: outputs/data-a-live-nezha/device/private/logs/1795299155 (kernel.txt, line 770+).

--- a/libkern/c++/sweet_nub.cpp
+++ b/libkern/c++/sweet_nub.cpp
@@ -226,6 +226,16 @@
 				*(void ***)mem = (void **)SWEET_NUB_CTL_VTABLE;
 				ctl = (IOService *)mem;
 				ctl->retain();
+				/*
+				 * 0520: the class's generated constructor does two things: sets the object's
+				 * retain count to 1 (what retain() just did) and calls
+				 * gMetaClass.instanceConstructed(). Skipping the second one makes the metaclass
+				 * count stay 0, so the first release() drives it to -1 and the kernel breaks:
+				 * the 0519 boot died at 10.195 s with "OSMetaClass: Class IOUSBDeviceController
+				 * - bad retain (-1)" right after the S2 sample tried to release the object.
+				 * instanceConstructed() is public (OSMetaClass.h) and propagates to superclasses.
+				 */
+				mc->instanceConstructed();
 				printf("SWEET NUB: manual -> %p, own class %s\n", ctl,
 				    ctl->getMetaClass()->getClassName());
 			}
